WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.554
In this film you set up a free working environment for FundsXML: the schema, the example files, and an editor that knows the standard.

2
00:00:08.604 --> 00:00:10.247
It takes about four minutes.

3
00:00:11.497 --> 00:00:13.737
Everything starts with the schema.

4
00:00:13.787 --> 00:00:17.913
It is published on GitHub under the MIT licence, in two forms.

5
00:00:17.963 --> 00:00:23.705
One flat file, FundsXML4.xsd, contains everything, and is the one to validate against.

6
00:00:23.755 --> 00:00:29.699
The modular form splits it into one file per area, which is easier to read and to change.

7
00:00:29.749 --> 00:00:35.155
The schema uses XSD version one point one, with assertions and conditional rules.

8
00:00:35.205 --> 00:00:43.574
So you need a validator that supports it, such as Saxon, Xerces, or the editor in this film; older tools like xmllint do not.

9
00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:49.653
FreeXmlToolkit is a free XML editor, and it has a FundsXML extension.

10
00:00:49.703 --> 00:00:51.073
It is off at first.

11
00:00:51.123 --> 00:00:56.308
Open a FundsXML file, and a bar above the editor offers to turn it on.

12
00:00:56.961 --> 00:00:59.236
Click Enable the FundsXML extension.

13
00:00:59.286 --> 00:01:06.339
FreeXmlToolkit fetches the official FundsXML schemas and example files from GitHub, and keeps them up to date.

14
00:01:06.389 --> 00:01:10.537
You can turn the extension off again at any time in the settings.

15
00:01:11.188 --> 00:01:13.228
Two things change right away.

16
00:01:13.278 --> 00:01:19.282
The status bar shows a green FundsXML mark: the document was recognised as FundsXML.

17
00:01:19.332 --> 00:01:23.534
And the activity bar on the left has a new FundsXML button.

18
00:01:24.186 --> 00:01:26.322
Click it to open the FundsXML panel.

19
00:01:26.372 --> 00:01:32.213
At the top, you choose the schema version, here four point two point eleven, the current release.

20
00:01:32.263 --> 00:01:34.825
Every check in the panel uses this version.

21
00:01:35.475 --> 00:01:47.236
Below are the tools for the open document: validate it against the FundsXML schema, run the data-quality checks, look up its identifiers online, and helpers for the grid view and for percentages.

22
00:01:47.286 --> 00:01:49.901
Later films in this series use each of them.

23
00:01:50.551 --> 00:01:59.905
Further down, the identifier checker for ISIN and LEI codes, and links to the schema, its documentation, and the folder with the example files.

24
00:02:00.557 --> 00:02:02.969
Now the most useful help while you work.

25
00:02:03.019 --> 00:02:06.540
Click into any element, here the asset type of a security.

26
00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:15.219
The Properties panel shows what the schema says about it: its data type, the allowed codes, its place in the document, and its documentation.

27
00:02:15.269 --> 00:02:21.991
The FundsXML schema documents every element, in English and in German, so the answer is always one click away.

28
00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:24.947
You can also read the schema itself.

29
00:02:24.997 --> 00:02:28.283
In the FundsXML panel, click Open Schema in Editor.

30
00:02:28.333 --> 00:02:31.162
The flat schema opens next to your document.

31
00:02:31.212 --> 00:02:38.228
The status bar shows it right away: an XSD one point one schema, so it needs an XSD one point one validator.

32
00:02:38.278 --> 00:02:44.639
Below, every element and type comes with its documentation, in English, German and more languages.

33
00:02:44.689 --> 00:02:50.528
Search it with Control F, or switch to the graphic view to see the structure as a diagram.

34
00:02:51.178 --> 00:02:52.171
One more place.

35
00:02:52.221 --> 00:03:00.933
Close all documents, and the Welcome page shows a FundsXML row: open an example, open the schema, or browse all example files.

36
00:03:00.983 --> 00:03:06.635
Click Open Example, and you start with a real FundsXML document, ready to explore.

37
00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:10.075
FreeXmlToolkit is not the only tool.

38
00:03:10.125 --> 00:03:17.545
On the command line, Saxon or Xerces validate files in scripts and build pipelines; both support XSD one point one.

39
00:03:17.595 --> 00:03:24.496
Your development environment, such as IntelliJ, VS Code or Eclipse, offers code completion from the schema.

40
00:03:24.546 --> 00:03:29.562
And the online documentation shows every element of the schema in the browser.

41
00:03:29.612 --> 00:03:33.655
Use whatever fits your work: the files are the same everywhere.

42
00:03:35.005 --> 00:03:36.673
Your environment is ready.

43
00:03:36.723 --> 00:03:42.290
In the next film, you take a FundsXML delivery apart, starting with its control data.

44
00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:43.612
Thanks for watching.
